17592186434465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434465 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8327a55a-2e34-5803-8492-faffa49567e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time until a laser fails, in hours, in a specific type of machine\n\nII. The number obtained when launching a fair roulette wheel\n\nIII. The number of children in a daycare center" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434466 #edb/ref 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Laser failure in a particular machine can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the time it takes for the laser to fail.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. Moreover, the number obtained when launching a roulette wheel is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of children is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time it takes for a taxi to reach any address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nII. The time it takes for a taxi to reach a particular address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nIII. The number of defective electronic components produced in a factory every week" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434467 #edb/ref 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow an exponential distribution. The times needed to reach different destinations will likely have different average rates.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Journeys along a particular route can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time needed to reach the destination.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective components is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time between customer calls, in minutes, for a particular business\n\nII. The number of winning lottery tickets in a set of $10$ tickets\n\nIII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $0$ and $15$ at random" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434468 #edb/ref 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled using an exponential distribution. Calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next call occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of winning lottery tickets is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number obtained is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
